</div><includeonly>{{#if:{{{1|}}}||[[Category:Pages with template errors]]}}{{#switch:{{{type|}}}| = | warning = | #default = [[Category:Pages with template errors]]}}</includeonly></onlyinclude><!--
  Two registers. Written plainly the box is an aside: here is something worth knowing on the way
  past. Written {{note|type=warning|1=...}} it is something the reader has to act on, and it says
  so with the warning colours and a different mark. Twelve of the fourteen uses in this
  documentation are asides, which is exactly why the two that are not have to look different: a
  box that looks the same either way teaches the reader to skim it, and the two that matter are
  then the two that get skimmed.

  The category is how a note with no text says so, and it is the one every template here files
  into rather than each keeping its own. Without it the placeholder above draws, the page builds,
  and nothing else says anything: an unnamed argument holding "=" or "|" is parsed as a named one,
  so {{note|a=b}} reaches {{{1}}} with nothing. Write that one {{note|1=a=b}}.

  A type that is neither empty nor "warning" files into the same category, because it would
  otherwise render as an aside and say nothing: {{note|type=warn|...}} is a warning the reader
  never sees. Adding a register means adding it to the switch as well as to the class and the
  icon above.

  WikvenFailOnCategories in .wikven.yaml names the category, so a build that fills it stops and
  says which page did. includeonly keeps this page itself out of it; it is outside onlyinclude
  so that no page carries it.
-->
